1
00:00:00,700 --> 00:00:05,238
Inspect the adapter that turns terminal interaction into a predictable test boundary.

2
00:00:06,100 --> 00:00:10,588
We will connect, wait for the correct panel, enter a transfer reference, parse the

3
00:00:10,600 --> 00:00:15,812
result, and close the session. Each step has a specific condition and a bounded

4
00:00:15,825 --> 00:00:21,388
lifetime. The public laboratory wrapper is named TerminalSession; it is our adapter,

5
00:00:21,625 --> 00:00:28,325
not an IBM library class. Its implementation uses real TNZ methods, so you can inspect

6
00:00:28,338 --> 00:00:32,650
the difference between application-specific screen logic and the underlying protocol

7
00:00:32,662 --> 00:00:33,212
client.

8
00:00:35,430 --> 00:00:41,305
Open the adapter source and find the TNZ object creation. One object represents this

9
00:00:41,317 --> 00:00:46,430
terminal connection. The adapter selects the character encoding used by the local

10
00:00:46,442 --> 00:00:52,317
simulator, so screen bytes become the intended text. A browser page and a terminal

11
00:00:52,330 --> 00:00:57,742
screen do not share a locator model or character buffer. Keep this conversion visible

12
00:00:57,755 --> 00:01:03,380
when diagnosing unexpected symbols or missing labels. The displayed initialization is

13
00:01:03,392 --> 00:01:08,092
an excerpt from the adapter; connection, bounded waiting, and cleanup belong to the

14
00:01:08,105 --> 00:01:11,142
following lifecycle rather than being optional additions.

15
00:01:13,376 --> 00:01:18,913
The adapter connects to loopback on the simulator's terminal port. Its host validation

16
00:01:18,926 --> 00:01:24,376
rejects nonlocal addresses before attempting this plaintext classroom connection. The

17
00:01:24,388 --> 00:01:29,213
secure flag shown here describes only that local setup; certificate verification has

18
00:01:29,226 --> 00:01:34,063
no effect when the connection is unencrypted. After connecting, the adapter

19
00:01:34,076 --> 00:01:39,376
immediately waits for the expected initial panel. A successful socket call is not

20
00:01:39,388 --> 00:01:44,813
enough to begin typing. If setup fails after connection, the session owner must still

21
00:01:44,826 --> 00:01:48,813
shut down the partially established connection before reporting the original error.

22
00:01:51,034 --> 00:01:56,384
Inspect the wait helper. It computes a deadline from a monotonic clock, reads the

23
00:01:56,396 --> 00:02:01,059
current screen, and processes incoming terminal events in short bounded increments.

24
00:02:01,922 --> 00:02:06,284
Repeating a screen read without processing events may keep observing stale content.

25
00:02:07,146 --> 00:02:11,434
The helper also checks whether the session disconnected and saves a timeout snapshot

26
00:02:11,446 --> 00:02:16,534
before raising an error. This is application-level waiting around the TNZ event

27
00:02:16,546 --> 00:02:22,059
method. The deadline expresses how long this local operation may take, and prevents a

28
00:02:22,072 --> 00:02:25,096
missing panel from hanging the test process indefinitely.

29
00:02:27,324 --> 00:02:32,299
The wait helper does not accept matching text alone. It also checks the terminal's

30
00:02:32,312 --> 00:02:37,424
program-wait and system-lock conditions before returning control to the caller. This

31
00:02:37,436 --> 00:02:41,674
prevents the test from typing while the displayed panel is still not ready for input.

32
00:02:42,536 --> 00:02:47,374
Treat those conditions as part of the adapter's readiness contract. If the label

33
00:02:47,386 --> 00:02:52,036
appears but input remains locked, inspect the terminal exchange and timeout evidence.

34
00:02:52,899 --> 00:02:57,486
Do not bypass the lock check merely because a screenshot looks complete; presentation

35
00:02:57,499 --> 00:02:59,362
and readiness can be different states.

36
00:03:01,574 --> 00:03:05,799
Before touching the field, validate the reference against the adapter's allowed length

37
00:03:05,812 --> 00:03:11,274
and characters. The local field accepts one to forty-eight ASCII letters, digits,

38
00:03:11,374 --> 00:03:16,999
underscores, or hyphens. Then move to the input field with the home key, erase its

39
00:03:17,011 --> 00:03:21,662
previous contents, and type the reference through TNZ. Clearing matters when a new

40
00:03:21,674 --> 00:03:26,186
reference is shorter than the old one; otherwise, stale characters can remain and

41
00:03:26,199 --> 00:03:31,524
select the wrong lookup value. This field sequence belongs to our panel and must be

42
00:03:31,536 --> 00:03:33,574
reviewed for any different application.

43
00:03:35,800 --> 00:03:40,400
After Enter, wait for a result screen containing the submitted reference and a status

44
00:03:40,412 --> 00:03:45,575
that is no longer the initial ready state. A generic completed message could belong to

45
00:03:45,588 --> 00:03:50,675
a previous lookup. Correlation therefore belongs in the screen predicate itself,

46
00:03:50,838 --> 00:03:56,225
before parsing amounts or declaring success. The helper still requires an unlocked

47
00:03:56,238 --> 00:04:01,662
terminal and a deadline. If the server returns not found or backend unavailable, the

48
00:04:01,675 --> 00:04:05,650
screen can be a valid response even though the business operation did not produce a

49
00:04:05,662 --> 00:04:07,175
successful lookup result.

50
00:04:09,402 --> 00:04:14,277
The adapter extracts values from labeled result lines and returns a structured object.

51
00:04:15,140 --> 00:04:19,377
For a completed lookup, it converts amount and balance into integers and preserves

52
00:04:19,390 --> 00:04:25,752
currency, source account, status, and reference. The hybrid test compares those values

53
00:04:25,764 --> 00:04:31,102
with the transfer it created; parsing is insufficient. Missing labels or invalid

54
00:04:31,114 --> 00:04:35,177
numbers should surface as errors rather than becoming empty strings or zeros that

55
00:04:35,190 --> 00:04:40,452
happen to satisfy a weak assertion. Review the parser alongside the actual screen

56
00:04:40,464 --> 00:04:43,314
contract whenever a field name or layout changes.

57
00:04:45,548 --> 00:04:50,798
Use the adapter's snapshot option to save the observed panels. The resulting text

58
00:04:50,810 --> 00:04:55,748
connects readiness and result states with named steps. Pair this snapshot with the

59
00:04:55,761 --> 00:05:00,348
structured JSON output so a reviewer can inspect both the parsed values and their

60
00:05:00,360 --> 00:05:05,336
source screen. The placeholder reference in the displayed command must be replaced

61
00:05:05,348 --> 00:05:10,410
with a real transfer identifier whose fixture still exists. Do not present the

62
00:05:10,423 --> 00:05:15,748
placeholder as a successful lookup. If the fixture was already deleted, a not-found

63
00:05:15,761 --> 00:05:18,148
result is the meaningful expected behavior.

64
00:05:20,374 --> 00:05:25,374
The session wrapper closes the TNZ connection on exit, including after a parsing or

65
00:05:25,386 --> 00:05:30,624
assertion failure. Its setup path also closes if the initial panel never becomes

66
00:05:30,636 --> 00:05:36,424
ready. Inspect both paths, because a finalization block around only the test body

67
00:05:36,436 --> 00:05:41,362
cannot clean up a resource that failed earlier during entry. The underlying close

68
00:05:41,374 --> 00:05:47,162
operation uses TNZ shutdown. Capture necessary screen evidence before releasing the

69
00:05:47,174 --> 00:05:51,774
session, and preserve the original failure if a secondary cleanup problem appears.

70
00:05:52,636 --> 00:05:55,712
Ownership should remain understandable across test cases.

71
00:05:57,936 --> 00:06:02,674
Run the terminal protocol tests for successful lookup, unknown reference, unavailable

72
00:06:02,686 --> 00:06:07,986
backend, invalid input, and a missing-screen timeout. These checks distinguish

73
00:06:07,999 --> 00:06:13,224
business data, missing records, dependency errors, validation, and bounded waiting.

74
00:06:14,086 --> 00:06:19,586
Inspect each actual result independently. The adapter's exit code of two represents a

75
00:06:19,599 --> 00:06:25,611
business response; code one represents an operational or validation failure. Interpret

76
00:06:25,624 --> 00:06:30,711
the structured status too. Treating every nonzero result as identical loses the

77
00:06:30,724 --> 00:06:33,974
information needed to diagnose a hybrid workflow efficiently.

78
00:06:36,194 --> 00:06:40,657
Your checkpoint is an explanation of the adapter's full contract: permitted reference

79
00:06:40,669 --> 00:06:45,719
input, loopback destination, ready-screen condition, field operations, result

80
00:06:45,732 --> 00:06:50,982
predicate, structured values, timeout behavior, and cleanup. Point to the

81
00:06:50,994 --> 00:06:56,107
implementation and one actual snapshot. Then explain why matching the reference

82
00:06:56,119 --> 00:07:01,494
matters before accepting a completed status. Call the adapter from a browser test

83
00:07:01,506 --> 00:07:06,556
without embedding terminal details everywhere. The next episode uses that boundary

84
00:07:06,569 --> 00:07:11,506
from Python and Java to compare the same synthetic transfer across web, service, and

85
00:07:11,519 --> 00:07:12,882
terminal observations.
